{
 "title": "Voltage Dividers, Briefly",
 "feedTitle": "Example Jekyll Blog",
 "url": "https://example.com/platform-math/",
 "contentHTML": "<p>A calm reading surface gives the words enough room to establish their own rhythm without adding another layer of interface.</p><p>The output of a divider is a fraction of its input:</p><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V = \\frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2} U</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.1em;vertical-align:-0.8em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">V</span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mfrac\">R2 R1+R2</span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">U</span></span></span></span></span><p>So with equal resistors, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V = U/2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">V</span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mord\">U/2</span></span></span></span>, half the input.</p><p>A calm reading surface gives the words enough room to establish their own rhythm without adding another layer of interface.</p>"
}